---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Medibank — Biometric Login | Kara Wheat">
  <main>
    <!-- Back nav -->
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12">
      <a href="/work" class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] text-black/60 hover:text-black transition-colors">
        ← Back
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12 pb-16">
      <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[14px] uppercase tracking-widest text-black/50 mb-4">
        Medibank — App Security
      </p>
      <h1 class="font-['Instrument_Serif'] text-[48px] leading-tight mb-8">
        Getting members to trust biometric login
      </h1>
      <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80 max-w-3xl">
        When Medibank introduced Multi-Factor Authentication, members who logged in manually
        faced SMS verification on every login. Members using biometrics only faced it
        periodically. That gap was the opportunity — and the question was whether a single
        prompt, shown at the right moment, could get people to take it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Placeholder image -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 mb-24">
      <div class="w-full aspect-video bg-black/5 border border-black/10 flex items-center justify-center">
        <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[14px] text-black/40">
          [Image: biometric-login-prompt-hero.png]
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Dilemma -->
    <section class="w-full py-24" style="background-color: #FFF8E1;">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
        <h2 class="font-['Instrument_Serif'] text-[48px] leading-tight mb-8">
          The Dilemma
        </h2>
        <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80 max-w-3xl">
          MFA made accounts safer, but it introduced real friction. Every manual login now
          meant waiting for a code, switching apps, and typing it back in. Biometric login
          removed that friction almost entirely — but nobody was choosing it. Members needed
          a reason to opt in, and it needed to appear at the exact moment the benefit was
          obvious: right after they'd just felt that friction.
        </p>
        <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80 max-w-3xl mt-6">
          The team's assumption was straightforward: a well-timed prompt after MFA login would
          lift biometric adoption. My job was to find out whether that assumption held up in
          practice, and if it didn't, why.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Approach -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-24">
      <h2 class="font-['Instrument_Serif'] text-[48px] leading-tight mb-8">
        The Approach
      </h2>
      <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80 max-w-3xl mb-12">
        Rather than just asking members if they'd enable biometrics, I wanted to watch what
        they actually did when placed in realistic, goal-driven scenarios — checking their
        digital card, finding a dentist, logging in while distracted. Moderated interviews
        against a Figma prototype, recruited through Askable, split evenly across age and
        gender. Existing Medibank app users only.
      </p>

      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h3 class="font-['Inter'] font-normal text-[16px] uppercase tracking-widest text-black/50 mb-6">
          What we set out to test
        </h3>
        <ul class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80 space-y-2 mb-12">
          <li>Would members in a hurry defer the prompt and close it?</li>
          <li>Would they understand how to dismiss it?</li>
          <li>Would they understand what checking "don't show again" actually meant?</li>
          <li>Did members understand what biometrics were, and what enabling them changed?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Findings -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 pb-24">
      <h2 class="font-['Instrument_Serif'] text-[48px] leading-tight mb-8">
        What We Learned
      </h2>

      <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-12">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-['Inter'] font-normal text-[16px] mb-3">Timing wasn't enough on its own</h3>
          <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80">
            Every participant described frequent, goal-oriented use of the app — checking
            Live Better points, finding a dentist, pulling up a digital card. Placed in that
            mindset, every single one closed the prompt immediately. The moment was right,
            but a one-off nudge wasn't going to compete with someone's actual task.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-['Inter'] font-normal text-[16px] mb-3">"Close" wasn't the right word</h3>
          <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80">
            Everyone could find the exit — the X icon and the "Close" button were both spotted
            instantly. But several participants expected softer, more deferential language:
            "Skip" or "Not now." "Close" worked functionally but read as more final than most
            people meant it to be.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-['Inter'] font-normal text-[16px] mb-3">The opt-out checkbox was ambiguous</h3>
          <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80">
            Most participants read "don't show this again" correctly as a permanent opt-out.
            Two didn't — they read it as confirmation they wanted to proceed, treating it as
            part of setup rather than a way out. Both suggested the same fix independently:
            wording closer to "No thanks" or "I'll pass."
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-['Inter'] font-normal text-[16px] mb-3">Members weren't one audience — they were three</h3>
          <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80">
            Biometrics-positive members already used Face ID or Touch ID everywhere and saw
            the upside immediately. Security-first members understood biometrics fully but
            were wary of irreversibility and breaches — MFA felt "good enough" to them.
            Biometrics-skeptical members had been burned by unreliable Face ID or Touch ID
            elsewhere and assumed it would be slower, not faster. One prompt couldn't speak
            to all three the same way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-['Inter'] font-normal text-[16px] mb-3">The real benefit wasn't landing</h3>
          <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80">
            Members agreed MFA was necessary but frustrating, and that removing repeated SMS
            checks was appealing. But several weren't sure that enabling biometrics was what
            actually reduced it — the connection between "turn this on" and "fewer SMS codes"
            needed to be stated, not implied.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Result -->
    <section class="w-full py-24" style="background-color: #FFF8E1;">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
        <h2 class="font-['Instrument_Serif'] text-[48px] leading-tight mb-8">
          The Result
        </h2>
        <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80 max-w-3xl mb-6">
          The research reshaped the prompt before it shipped: dismissal language changed from
          "Close" to softer, lower-commitment wording; the opt-out checkbox copy was rewritten
          to remove the ambiguity that tripped up a fifth of participants; and the benefit
          statement was made explicit — enabling biometrics was tied directly to fewer SMS
          checks, not left for members to infer.
        </p>
        <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80 max-w-3xl">
          On Android, the share of logins using biometrics rose from 56.3% to 61.3% over the
          tracked period — a 4% lift on the previous ten-week high. The trend dipped briefly
          mid-period, but that was traced to an unrelated MFA login bug rather than the
          prompt itself, and adoption recovered once it was resolved.
        </p>
        <p class="font-['Inter'] font-light text-[16px] leading-[36px] text-black/80 max-w-3xl mt-6">
          The prompt's own interaction data backed this up directly: most members who engaged
          with it chose "go to settings" over "don't show this again," by a margin of roughly
          two to one. That interaction spike lined up closely with a jump in the app's device
          authentication toggle — enable actions on the settings page rose from 18.5% to a
          peak of 40.8% of settings interactions in the weeks the prompt was live.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>